<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n32095" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">occĭno</span></b> , ŭi
			(<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>perf.</i>
			occecini, <i><span class="en">Liv. 6, 41, 8</span></i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">occanui,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Serv. ad Verg. G. 2, 38, 4</span></i>
			), 3, v. n.
			ob-cano. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>To sing</i> or <i>chirp
			inauspiciously</i>, <i>to croak</i>, etc. (not in
			Cic. or Cæs.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">si occinuerit avis,</span>”
				i. e. <i>if by its chirping it gives an unfavorable
					omen</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Liv.
					6, 41, 8</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">corvus voce clarā occinuit,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					10, 40 <i>fin.</i></span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">occinentes in eum adversum corvi,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Val.
					Max. 1, 4, 2</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> In
			gen., <i>to sing</i>, <i>chirp</i>, <i>cry</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">animalia inter se,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">App. Flor. p. 349, 20</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
